# Copy to config.env and edit.
#
# The bash tools load this via tools/_config.sh; tools/investigate loads it itself.
# An explicit environment variable always beats the value here, so a one-off
# `MEM_WARN_MB=99999 tools/watchdog` still works - which is also how you prove a
# check fires at all.
#
# If you add a tool, wire it up. This file used to claim every script sourced it
# while most did not: tools/watchdog carried its own hardcoded data paths and
# ignored DATA_ROOT, so relocating the data would have left it watching an empty
# directory and calling the pipeline healthy.

# ---- Router (capture sensor) -------------------------------------------------
ROUTER_HOST=192.168.1.1
ROUTER_USER=admin
# Interface to capture on. Use the LAN bridge, not the WAN interface: the bridge
# carries every routed flow and preserves pre-NAT LAN source addresses, which the
# WAN side has already masqueraded behind a single IP.
CAPTURE_IFACE=br0

# ---- Collector ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The address the router sees the collector as. Used to build the BPF rule that
# excludes the capture transport from its own capture.
COLLECTOR_IP=192.168.1.100

# 0 = full packet capture. 512 keeps headers, DNS and TLS SNI at roughly 10-20x
# less volume - enough to answer "who talked to what", not enough for payload search.
SNAPLEN=0
# Seconds per PCAP file. Ingest latency is roughly one rotation.
#
# This is a two-sided trade and BOTH sides have bitten. Do not tune it against
# one of them alone.
#
# Too long -> outsized captures. A burst inside one rotation window produces a
# file an order of magnitude past the norm, and a single oversized file
# deadlocks the downstream mover. Measured at 300s: median 0.9 MB, p95 38 MB,
# and a 156 MB file that stalled the pipeline for over an hour.
#
# Too short -> more files, forever. pcap/processed is polled with a full
# scandir+stat every cycle, so scan cost tracks FILE COUNT, and the count at
# steady state is (86400 / ROTATE_SECS) * PCAP_RETENTION_DAYS. Measured on the
# Docker Desktop bind mount at 1.14 ms/entry: 300s/14d = 4,032 files = ~4.6 s
# per poll; 60s/14d = 20,160 files = ~23 s per poll, against a 10 s settle
# window. That is how the watcher goes quiet with no error and no open sockets.
#
# 120s with PCAP_RETENTION_DAYS=7 sits between them: ~15 MB captures and ~5,040
# files (~5.8 s per poll). Change one and check the other.
#
# tcpdump -C (size-based rotation) is NOT the fix: it appends a numeric suffix
# (file.pcap1, file.pcap2) which breaks the .pcap extension the pipeline matches
# on, so those captures are ignored entirely.
ROTATE_SECS=120
# Router-side libpcap buffer in KiB. Raise if tcpdump reports kernel drops.
RBUF=8192
# Captures above this are quarantined rather than queued (see sensor/rotate.sh).
MAX_PCAP_BYTES=52428800

# ---- Malcolm -----------------------------------------------------------------
MALCOLM_VERSION=v26.07.1
MALCOLM_DIR="${HOME}/malcolm"
MALCOLM_ADMIN_USER=admin

# JVM heaps. Malcolm's configure sizes these against *total* system RAM, which
# is the wrong input twice over: it ignores what is actually free, and it
# ignores how much data you have. It handed OpenSearch a 6g heap (7.6 GB
# resident) for a 160 MB index, which pushed the host into swap and correlated
# with watcher threads silently wedging.
#
# Size against the INDEX, not the machine: roughly 1 GB of heap per 20-30 GB of
# index, with a floor of ~2g. 4g comfortably covers a 90-day retention target at
# home-network volumes. Check yours with:
#   tools/osapi es GET '_cluster/stats?filter_path=indices.store.size_in_bytes'
OPENSEARCH_HEAP=4g
LOGSTASH_HEAP=2048m

# Keep this many GB free before Arkime starts deleting the oldest PCAP.
ARKIME_FREESPACEG=500

# Enable OpenSearch Dashboards' enhanced Discover experience: multiple data
# sources, workspaces, and the Explore UI. Adds navigation Malcolm's curated
# dashboards do not use, so set false if you prefer the stock layout.
OSD_ENHANCED_DISCOVER=true

# ---- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Bulk data (PCAP, OpenSearch indices, Zeek/Suricata logs). Full-packet capture
# runs tens of GB per day - put this on your largest volume.
DATA_ROOT=/d/malcolm-data

# ---- Docker Desktop on Windows ----------------------------------------------
# Set true when the Docker engine is Docker Desktop backed by a Windows host.
# It changes how bind-mount sources are written, which is not cosmetic: relative
# and Linux-style paths resolve to a DIFFERENT, EMPTY view of the filesystem, so
# containers silently start against empty directories.
DOCKER_DESKTOP_WINDOWS=true
# Windows-side location of MALCOLM_DIR, in Docker Desktop notation.
MALCOLM_DIR_WIN=//c/Users/YOU/malcolm
# Windows-side location of DATA_ROOT.
DATA_ROOT_WIN=//d/malcolm-data
# Windows-side location of this repo's key directory (mounted into the sensor).
KEYS_DIR_WIN=//c/Users/YOU/er4-malcolm-nsm/keys

# ---- TLS interception (optional) --------------------------------------------
# Off by default. This puts a proxy in the path of real traffic - read
# docs/MITM.md before enabling.
#
# proxy       explicit HTTP proxy only. Nothing is redirected; you point a
#             browser at it deliberately. Start here.
# transparent tunnel + local REDIRECT, router policy-routes clients in.
MITM_MODE=proxy
MITM_LISTEN_PORT=8080
MITM_WEB_PORT=8081
MITM_INTERCEPT_PORTS="80 443"
# Decrypted flows are exported as NDJSON and shipped into OpenSearch by
# tools/mitm-ingest. (Not via a TLS keylog - mitmproxy writes none for sessions
# it terminates, and Arkime cannot consume one. Both were tested.)
MITM_CA_DIR_WIN=//c/Users/YOU/er4-malcolm-nsm/mitm/ca
MITM_FLOWS_DIR_WIN=//d/malcolm-data/mitm/flows

# Body capture.
MITM_MAX_BODY=8192
MITM_CAPTURE_BODIES=true

# Credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Set-Cookie, X-API-Key, ...).
# true  = stored as <redacted>
# false = stored verbatim. Full fidelity for forensics or debugging your own
#         auth flows - and the flow index then holds live session tokens that
#         anyone with OpenSearch read access can replay. Each document records
#         which mode it was captured under (mitm.redacted).
MITM_REDACT_HEADERS=true
# Extra headers to redact, comma separated.
MITM_REDACT_EXTRA=""

# Restrict interception to specific clients rather than the whole LAN - fewer
# pinned apps break, and a smaller index of sensitive material. Empty means the
# routing script refuses to run rather than defaulting to everything.
MITM_CLIENTS="192.168.1.100"

# Transparent-mode routing (router/setup-mitm-route.sh). The router MARKs
# matching traffic and policy-routes it down the tunnel; it never DNATs, because
# that would destroy the original destination the proxy needs.
MITM_TUNNEL_IF=awg0
MITM_ROUTE_TABLE=101
MITM_FWMARK=0x4d
# Far side of the tunnel, if it cannot be derived from the interface route.
MITM_PEER_IP=""
# Where clients should send proxied traffic in proxy/WPAD mode.
MITM_PROXY_HOST=""

# ---- Observer self-identification --------------------------------------------
# Destinations belonging to whatever is doing the monitoring - agent sessions,
# remote shells, log shippers. They egress from the network they are watching
# and are frequently its largest flow (measured: 79% of all upload), which reads
# as exfiltration until labelled. Comma-separated IPs.
OBSERVER_DESTS=""

# ---- PCAP retention ----------------------------------------------------------
# Raw captures are time-bound, separately from session metadata (which has its
# own 90-day ISM policy). Pruning here costs the ability to pull raw packets for
# an old session; the session and every dashboard still work.
#
# Time, not size, because pcap/processed is polled on every pipeline cycle and
# scan cost tracks file COUNT. A size cap of 300 GB still leaves ~56,000 files.
#
# This is coupled to ROTATE_SECS - the two multiply into the polled file count.
# Read the note there before raising either.
PCAP_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# Captures quarantined by sensor/rotate.sh get their own, longer bound. Nothing
# polls that directory and nothing else prunes it, so without this it grows
# forever. Longer than the routine archive because these are rare, anomalous,
# never analysed, and the only copy of whatever burst produced them.
QUARANTINE_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---- Watchdog ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory headroom below which tools/watchdog warns. This is not a tidiness check:
# OpenSearch reports an out-of-memory HOST as a CORRUPT INDEX - a flush fails to
# read its segment infos and Lucene raises CorruptIndexException whose innermost
# cause is "Cannot allocate memory". The shard is then unassignable and that data
# is gone. It happened here at 143 MB available with 13.5 GB of 16 GB swap in use,
# and the first sign of trouble was a red cluster. Warning early is the only way
# to act before the loss, and the next index hit may be a session index rather
# than a stats one.
MEM_WARN_MB=2048
SWAP_WARN_PCT=85
#
# TUNE THESE TO YOUR HOST, and measure rather than guess. The method:
#
#   1. Record what the host looks like during NORMAL operation, including
#      whatever else shares it - sample `free -m` for a minute or two under a
#      typical load, and take the FLOOR, not the average.
#   2. Set MEM_WARN_MB just below that floor. Above it and the check fires
#      constantly; far below it and the warning arrives too late to act on.
#
# On the host this was written for, that produced MEM_WARN_MB=1400 with
# SWAP_WARN_PCT effectively disabled at 99, because **swap% turned out to be
# anti-correlated**: routine operation ran at 94% while the corruption that
# motivated the check happened at 83%. A threshold that only ever fires on
# healthy states is worse than no threshold - it trains you to ignore the alert
# that matters. Check whether swap discriminates on YOUR host before trusting it.
#
# The uncomfortable part is worth stating plainly: on a host that shares memory
# with heavy jobs, the gap between "normal" and "an index was destroyed" can be a
# couple of hundred MB. The check is then a LATE warning. A quiet watchdog is not
# evidence of headroom, and the real fix is not to run both on one box.

# How long a restarted consumer is left alone before "no events" counts as a
# failure. pcap_watcher runs --start-sleep 60 and the mover --start-sleep 30, so a
# fresh restart is silent BY DESIGN; without a floor here the watchdog reads that
# designed silence as "the restart did not help" and escalates to a re-queue that
# was never needed. Seen at 5 seconds, when two checks interleaved.
RESTART_GRACE_SECS=120
# Once a restart has had its chance and the stall persists, stop restarting: a
# second restart only re-enters --start-sleep and discards the progress the first
# one made. That is the restart loop documented in docs/GOTCHAS.md.
RESTART_ESCALATE_SECS=900
# Publisher is considered stalled when the newest capture is this far ahead of the
# last publish.
STALL_SECS=420

# ---- GeoIP -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logstash's vendored GeoLite2 databases were built 2019-11-18. Reallocated
# ranges therefore resolve to their previous owners - two Hetzner ranges read as
# Iranian ISPs here. tools/update-geoip installs a current DB-IP ASN database
# (no licence key needed). See docs/GOTCHAS.md before trusting any country field.
GEOIP_DIR_WIN=//d/malcolm-data/geoip
